+- **minutes to restore.** The budget is the RTO an operator plans with, and the only honest form of
+  it is a measurement with the machine named — [the DR page](dr-drill.md) has one, on an Apple M2
+  with a debug build: 1.02 s for a 2.4 MB store, and 18–20 ms for the drill's own 569-byte copy. A
+  restore that takes longer than the budget is a restore nobody has practised, and the fix is
+  stage 4 rather than a faster disk.
 
 ## What happens when a budget is spent
 
-- [TODO](TODO)
+- **a refusal budget is spent** — stop adding series before changing the cap. The cap is a policy
+  about state, and raising it is the one change that makes the number look better while the store
+  goes on paying for it; `lakeletq inspect series --store $LAKELET_STORE` is what that decision is
+  made from, and [the cardinality page](cardinality.md) is the argument;
+- **the records-lost budget is spent** — the incident becomes a data-loss incident. Restore the
+  newest copy that verifies into a directory that is not the store (stage 3), say which span is
+  missing before anything is swapped in, and write the loss into the postmortem, because the write
+  path's policy is what has to change;
+- **the minutes-to-restore budget is spent** — rehearse, do not optimise: the copy that has never
+  been restored is the one that will be slow, and stage 4 is the rehearsal rather than an experiment
+  on the store being served.
+
+## What this page does not do
+
+It does not invent a dashboard, it does not promise a query latency, and it does not count a number
+this project does not print. Every counter above is read out of a log line or a command a page
+names, which is what makes each of them something an operator can check during an incident with the
+runbook open beside this page.
 
 ## Where the numbers are kept true
 
